body.explanation:
Read this complete program:

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Hello, CForge!\n");
    return 0;
}

Line 1 is a preprocessor directive. It copies declarations from the standard header stdio.h so printf is known. It is not a C statement; it has no semicolon.

int main(void) starts a function named main that returns an int and takes no parameters. void inside the parentheses is standard C and means 'no arguments'. Some old books write main() with an empty pair; this academy prefers int main(void).

The opening { begins the body. printf(...) is a statement: call the library function and pass a string. \n ends the line on the screen. The semicolon ends the statement. return 0; ends main successfully. The closing } matches the opening brace.

You may add declarations at the start of a block: int year = 2026; In C99 and C11 (our judge uses c11) you may also declare nearer first use. Keep beginners' declarations at the top of main if your teacher asks for that style.

Comments: /* this is ignored */ can span lines and is in every C standard. // this is ignored to the end of the line is standard from C99. Both are fine under -std=c11. They are not compiler-only extensions.

A program with no main will not link as a hosted student program. A missing semicolon is a compile error. A missing } is a compile error. Those messages come from the judge, not from an AI.

